        model:
          'In 1450, Western Europe was peripheral to world trade, while the true centres of global wealth and power lay in Ming China, the Islamic World, and West Africa.\n\nFirstly, Ming Dynasty China was the world’s most technologically advanced and commercially dominant superpower. Under the early Ming emperors, China produced the world\'s most sought-after manufactured commodities—including high-grade porcelain, fine silk, and tea. Between 1405 and 1433, Admiral Zheng He commanded seven massive maritime expeditions across the Indian Ocean to East Africa and the Persian Gulf. Zheng He’s armada of over 300 vessels, featuring nine-masted "Treasure Ships" four times larger than European caravels, demonstrated that China possessed unmatched naval engineering and economic supremacy without needing to conquer foreign territories by force.\n\nSecondly, the Ottoman Empire and Islamic trading networks controlled the vital economic crossroads between East and West. By conquering Constantinople in 1453 under Sultan Mehmed II, the Ottomans extinguished the Byzantine Empire and established total command over the overland Silk Road transit points and the Black Sea. European merchants from Venice and Genoa were forced to pay heavy customs duties and transit taxes to Ottoman gatekeepers. Europe lacked high-value manufactured goods that Eastern merchants desired, leaving European states with a constant deficit of silver and gold.\n\nFinally, West African kingdoms such as the Songhai and Mali Empires possessed vast mineral wealth that dwarfed European reserves. West Africa supplied the Mediterranean world with two-thirds of its gold through trans-Saharan caravan routes connecting cities like Timbuktu and Gao. By contrast, 15th-century Europe was a politically fractured continent still recovering from the devastation of the Black Death, characterized by localized feudal agriculture rather than global commercial power.\n\nIn conclusion, Europe in 1450 was not the centre of global wealth and power; it was an economically isolated periphery. European oceanic voyages in the late 15th century were launched not out of pre-existing supremacy, but out of desperate necessity to circumvent the Ottoman blockade and access the boundless wealth of Asia and Africa.',
