        model:
          '**Exemplar Model Answer (12 Marks - Historical Interpretation)**\n\nHistorians have long debated whether the Gunpowder Plot of 1605 was an authentic, fanatical Catholic terror conspiracy or an elaborate piece of state entrapment orchestrated by King James I’s Spymaster, Robert Cecil. While Interpretation 2 raises intriguing questions regarding Cecil’s intelligence network and political opportunism, I agree to a very large extent with Interpretation 1 (Antonia Fraser) that the plot was an authentic, independently conceived conspiracy that nearly succeeded in decapitating the Jacobean state.\n\nInterpretation 1 is powerfully supported by substantive historical evidence demonstrating the genuine agency and religious radicalism of the conspirators. Following decades of crushing £20 monthly recusancy fines and King James’s bitter betrayal in February 1604—when he expelled all Catholic priests—Robert Catesby was consumed by the belief that only an apocalyptic military strike could save English Catholicism. The logistics of the conspiracy were thoroughly practical and executed by the plotters themselves: Guy Fawkes was specifically recruited for his professional decade of sapping experience in the Spanish Army of Flanders, while Thomas Percy utilized his aristocratic position to lease the undercroft directly beneath the House of Lords. Furthermore, the conspirators smuggled 36 barrels of gunpowder across the Thames and orchestrated a synchronized armed rising in the Midlands to kidnap Princess Elizabeth. Most decisively, when cornered at Holbeche House on 8 November 1605, Catesby and Percy fought to the death rather than surrender, providing undeniable proof of their genuine fanaticism.\n\nConversely, Interpretation 2 (Francis Edwards) argues that the plot was largely manipulated, if not engineered, by Robert Cecil, Earl of Salisbury. Proponents of the entrapment thesis point out that in Jacobean England, the manufacture and sale of gunpowder was a strictly regulated government monopoly; it seems incredible that 2.5 tonnes of explosives could be transported through London streets without Cecil’s extensive spy network noticing. Furthermore, Cecil sat on the anonymous Monteagle Letter for ten days between 26 October and 5 November, deliberately staging a midnight discovery so King James could appear to have divinely unraveled the mystery. Cecil undeniably reaped immense political dividends from the crisis: the plot permanently blackened English Catholicism, enabled Parliament to pass the draconian Popish Recusants Act 1606, secured vital parliamentary subsidies, and cemented Cecil’s position as the indispensable master of state security.\n\nIn conclusion, while Interpretation 2 correctly demonstrates that Robert Cecil masterfully monitored, delayed, and politically exploited the plot, Interpretation 1 remains far more convincing. The entrapment thesis relies heavily on circumstantial suspicion rather than hard archival documentation. More crucially, allowing 36 barrels of live gunpowder to sit directly beneath King James, his heirs, and the entire ruling class would have been an unthinkably reckless gamble for Cecil, who could not guarantee that a single stray spark would not blow the regime to pieces. Therefore, Antonia Fraser’s orthodox interpretation is correct: the Gunpowder Plot was an authentic, lethal conspiracy born of religious volatility, which Cecil ruthlessly capitalized upon to secure Protestant supremacy.',
